Cell = [number, number]; // [x, y]\n\nfunction countNeighbors(cells: Cell[], cell: Cell): number {\n  const [x, y] = cell;\n  let count = 0;\n  for (const other of cells) {\n    if (other[0] === x && other[1] === y) continue;\n    const [ox, oy] = other;\n    if (Math.abs(ox - x) <= 1 && Math.abs(oy - y) <= 1) {\n      count++;\n    }\n  }\n  return count;\n}\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const result: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const cell of cells) {\n    const neighbors = countNeighbors(cells, cell);\n    if (neighbors === 2) {\n      result.push(cell);\n    }\n  }\n  return result;\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784980587361},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good — the implementation only keeps cells with exactly 2 neighbors. The test fails because `(1,1)` has 3 neighbors and doesn't get preserved, while `(0,1)` and `(2,1)` have 2 neighbors and get preserved. I need the test to properly represent \"3 neighbors → survives\" but without letting the other cells survive with 2 neighbors. \n\nLet me use a different configuration where only the center survives: a vertical line of 3 cells where middle has 2 neighbors (already tested), and then a 3-neighbor test. For 3 neighbors: use a setup where the center has 3 neighbors but those neighbors don't have 2-3 neighbors.\n\nBest approach: T-shape with center at `(1,1)` and arms at `(0,1)`, `(2,1)`, `(1,2)`. \n- `(1,1)`: neighbors = `(0,1)`, `(2,1)`, `(1,2)` = 3 → survives\n- `(0,1)`: neighbors = `(1,1)`, `(1,2)` (diagonal) = 2 → also survives\n- `(2,1)`: neighbors = `(1,1)`, `(1,2)` (diagonal) = 2 → also survives\n- `(1,2)`: neighbors = `(1,1)`, `(0,1)` (diagonal), `(2,1)` (diagonal) = 3 → survives\n\nStill all survive. The issue is geometric — it's hard to have a cell with 3 neighbors where those neighbors don't have each other. Let me just accept the expected to include all cells that survive and make the test check only that `(1,1)` (the 3-neighbor one) is in the result